Draw an **overworld terrain tileset**, a 96x96 sprite sheet for a top-down RPG,
using only the drawing tool.

The sheet is a **3x3 grid of nine 32x32 terrain tiles** — grass, water, sandy
beach, dirt path, dense forest canopy, rocky mountain, tilled crop field, a
shallow-water edge, and a stone bridge — each drawn so it **tiles seamlessly**, in
a bright storybook palette. It is the ground the whole world is painted from, so
every tile has to read clearly for what it is and repeat without a seam.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the image by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced
any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is discarded
and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and
appends the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
draw fill-rect --x 0 --y 0 --width 32 --height 32 --color "#7ec850"
```

Run `draw --help` to list every operation and `draw <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start — it fixes the grid
layout, the tile assignments, and the palette — and produce an implementation that
matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the tileset up operation by operation until the image in `canvas.png`
matches the brief — nine distinct, instantly-readable terrains laid out in the 3x3
grid, each one seamlessly tileable, in the bright storybook palette. When you are
satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json` is your finished submission.
